WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:01.910
- [Instructor] We're
already somewhat familiar

00:00:01.910 --> 00:00:04.240
with the idea of division.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.510
If I were to say eight divided by two

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:10.650
you could think of that as eight objects.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:12.440
So one, two, three,

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.210
four, five, six,

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:15.690
seven, eight

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:19.940
divided into equal groups of two.

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:22.640
And so how many equal groups
of two could you have?

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.440
Well you could have one,

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:25.620
two,

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:26.610
three,

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:29.030
or four groups of two

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:31.900
and so you'd say eight divided
by two is equal to four.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:33.850
Another way we could've thought about that

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:36.070
is you have one, two, three, four,

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:38.119
five, six, seven, eight

00:00:38.119 --> 00:00:41.090
and if you were to divide
it into two equal groups

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:43.660
well you could have one group of four.

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:44.870
Let me get a little bit cleaner.

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:48.560
One group of four and then
a second group of four.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:49.870
So two equal groups.

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:51.650
How many in each of those equal groups?

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:53.530
Well they're four in each of those groups.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:56.880
And so once again eight divided
by two is equal to four.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.080
Now we're going to extend
our knowledge of division

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:03.690
by starting to think about
things that don't divide evenly.

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:05.360
So what if we were to say

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:06.860
what is

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:11.250
eight divided by three?

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:12.210
Pause this video

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:15.120
and see if you can think
about that a little bit.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:18.690
All right so let's draw
eight objects again.

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:20.990
One, two, three, four,

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:23.830
five, six, seven, eight

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:25.630
and one way to think about it

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:27.090
is how we thought about it here

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:30.380
is can we divide this into groups

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:31.780
that all have three in them

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:34.520
and how many groups would
we be able to make of three?

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:35.830
Well let's try it out.

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:38.930
I can make this group of three,

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:42.320
I can make this second group of three

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:45.050
but I can't make any more group of three

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:49.260
and what I have left over are these two.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:51.720
And so the way that
you would describe this

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:53.470
or one way to describe this is

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:58.070
hey I was able to make two group of three

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:00.040
so it's equal to two

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:02.040
and there's some left over,

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:03.860
there's a remainder.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:05.090
Let me write that down.

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:06.480
Important concept.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.420
There is a remainder of two as well

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:13.150
and so sometimes it's
written as just a lowercase r

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:15.960
or remainder of two.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:17.690
Another way to think about it is

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:20.690
two, this two times three is six

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:22.620
and then if you were to
put back that remainder

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:24.520
that's how you can get to eight.

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:25.880
Now another way you can think about it

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:27.520
is how we thought about
it in the second example

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:29.100
with eight divided by two.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:30.490
Let me draw eight objects again.

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:32.560
One, two, three, four,

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:34.590
five, six, seven, eight

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:35.770
and you could say hey

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:39.780
let me divide that eight
into three equal groups.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:42.050
So pause this video and
see if you can divide this

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:43.670
into three equal groups

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:45.863
and then what might be left over.

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:49.430
All right, so I'm going to try

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:52.490
to divide this into three equal groups.

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:55.550
I'm not going to be able to put
four in each of those groups

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:58.270
'cause I can only make
two equal groups of four.

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:01.070
I'm not going to be able
to put three into those,

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:02.040
three equal groups

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.910
'cause I would actually be
outing nine for doing that

00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:07.230
so each of my groups are
going to have to be two.

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:09.960
So I could make one group of two,

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:11.680
another group of two,

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.870
and there you go three
equal groups of two.

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:16.570
So I was able to sort out
three equal groups of two

00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:18.000
with just these six

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.600
but once again I have a remainder.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:23.080
I'm not able to make use of these two,

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:25.510
they're not able to fit into one of

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:28.500
in this case one of
the three equal groups.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.720
If I said four equal groups
then they would fit in,

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:32.190
but if I just said three equal groups

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:33.510
'cause I'm dividing by three

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:36.350
then I have this left over again.

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:38.090
Let's do one more example.

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:42.470
What if I were to ask you what is,

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:43.930
what is

00:03:43.930 --> 00:03:45.560
13

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:47.790
divided by

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:49.790
divided by

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:51.860
four?

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:53.370
Pause this video and think about it

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:55.090
and as you might imagine

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:56.983
there will be a remainder involved.

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:01.830
All right, well let's draw 13 objects,

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:05.860
one, two, three, four,
five, six, seven, eight

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:09.260
nine, ten, eleven, twelve, thirteen

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:14.050
and we could try to divide
this into equal groups of four,

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:16.250
that's one way to think
about it so let's see.

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:19.780
That's a group of four,
I have one group of four.

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:22.700
That's a group of four,
I have two groups of four

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:24.700
and then that is a group of four

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:28.650
so I'm able to find three
equal groups of four

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:30.010
so this is equal to three.

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:30.843
Another way to think about it

00:04:30.843 --> 00:04:33.760
four goes into 13 three times

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:36.530
but then I have this
little lonely circle here,

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:37.770
I have one left over

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:40.560
I have a remainder of one

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.710
because four times three
that gets you to 12

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:44.970
but then if you wanna get to 13

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:47.663
well then you gotta throw
in that remainder there.

